+CLS applies variable-neighborhood descent using relocate, swap, 2-opt,
+Or-opt chains of length two and three, inter-route tail exchange, swap-star,
+and intra-route three- and four-edge reconnections, in that order. After an
+accepted move, the search restarts at the first operator. The archived
+configuration specifies 1{,}000 outer iterations; the implementation stops
+at this cap or when one pass finds no improvement. The higher-order
+operators sample additional breakpoints, so termination is not a certificate
+of local optimality over all moves in these neighborhoods. An exception
+inside the search loop also terminates it, returning the original input
+tour rather than the partially improved plan.
+
+CLS scores moves by travel distance, with capacity checked when evaluating
+moves. For fixed collected quantities, positive transport cost and no
+per-vehicle charge, as in the reported profit accounting, reducing distance
+also increases daily profit: the revenue term is constant. CLS does not
+optimize which bins to collect. It can transfer bins between routes,
+changing how the served set is partitioned across trips.
 
 \paragraph{Fast-TSP.}
-A narrower operation: holding each route's bin assignment fixed, find the best
-visiting order within it. Routes of up to roughly twenty stops are solved to
-optimality by dynamic programming; longer ones fall back to a stochastic local
-search within a small fixed budget. Fast-TSP cannot move a bin between routes,
-drop a bin, or account for revenue. It solves each route's ordering globally,
-whereas CLS improves the plan incrementally. Sect.~\ref{sec:res-improvers}
-compares configurations using the two improvers.
+Fast-TSP reorders each depot-to-depot route independently while holding its
+assigned bins fixed. The wrapper includes the depot in the distance
+submatrix and rounds scaled distances to integers. The nominal scaling
+factor is 10{,}000; the revised wrapper reduces it when necessary to keep
+integer distances within the library's 16-bit range, and retains the input
+order if the library call fails. These safeguards apply to future runs.
+The library uses exact dynamic programming for small instances and
+time-budgeted stochastic search for larger ones. Exactness applies to the
+integer-distance problem; global optimality is not claimed for every route.
+The archived configuration specifies a 30-second search budget per route,
+not per daily plan. Fast-TSP cannot transfer bins between routes or change
+the collected set. Its library call exposes no seed parameter, so the
+wrapper's seed argument does not make the stochastic search repeatable.
+Sect.~\ref{sec:res-improvers} compares configurations using the two improvers.
 
